                  },
                  "text": "Effect on Drug A: The enzyme which is inhibited may be responsible for converting a pro-drug to an active drug, and if that reaction is inhibited, the pro-drug version of Drug A would remain inactive\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Effect on Drug B: The enzyme may be responsible for changing an active drug to an inactive drug, and if that reaction is inhibited, the active drug version of Drug B may remain active for longer than expected\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, if the enzyme that was inadvertently affected is not involved in the metabolism of Drug C, there will be no effect on the metabolism of Drug C. Grapefruit actually affects enzymes that metabolise 50% of drugs. For the other 50%, it should have no effect.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed, If the enzyme that was inadvertently affected is not involved in the metabolism of Drug C, obviously there will be no effect on the metabolism of Drug C. Grapefruit actually affects enzymes that metabolise 50% of drugs. For the other 50%, it should have no effect.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                  },
                  "text": "Effect on Drug C: The particular enzyme affected is not involved in the metabolism of the drug, and that will result in respiratory distress, coma and death. &nbsp;\n"
                }
